steps.text:
Put the pieces together with the course's grading rules:

- a handful of trials, mostly fewer than 15 people per group;
- mostly trained men;
- mostly not randomised, quality about 4 out of 10;
- 1–12 weeks, so nothing on whole seasons;
- lab outcomes, not races; one null crossover trial.

That makes "blocks beat traditional" **weak** evidence [[c:per-ev-weak]]. Reverse periodisation is also weak (no advantage). The fair practical reading: a HIIT block is a reasonable way to vary training and may give a small extra gain over a few weeks, not a proven upgrade, and there's no evidence on whether it adds up over a season.
